            <ArticleTitle>Predictors and outcomes of ad hoc versus non-ad hoc percutaneous coronary interventions.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>350-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jcin.2009.01.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Our aim was to compare longer-term outcomes for ad hoc percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) and non-ad hoc PCI.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Ad hoc PCIs, whereby PCI is performed immediately after cardiac catheterization, has become the most common way of performing PCI. However, no studies have compared longer-term outcomes for ad hoc and non-ad hoc PCIs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 46,565 New York State patients who underwent PCI in nonfederal New York State hospitals between January 1, 2003 and June 30, 2005 were followed through December 31, 2005, and in-hospital and longer-term outcomes were compared for ad hoc and non-ad hoc PCI patients after adjusting for differences in pre-procedural risk factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was no difference in risk-adjusted in-hospital mortality (adjusted ad hoc/non-ad hoc odds ratio: 0.82, 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.55 to 1.22). Ad hoc PCI patients had significantly lower 36-month mortality (adjusted hazard ratio [HR]: 0.76, 95% CI: 0.69 to 0.85, p &lt; 0.0001). Ad hoc PCI patients had significantly higher 36-month subsequent revascularization (adjusted HR: 1.11, 95% CI: 1.01 to 1.21, p = 0.03), but after excluding subsequent PCIs that occurred within 30 days of the index PCI in another vessel, the difference was no longer significant (adjusted HR: 1.03, 95% CI: 0.95 to 1.12, p = 0.43).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">On average, lower-risk patients undergo ad hoc PCI, and after risk-adjustment for differences in patient mix, ad hoc PCI patients have lower 3-year mortality rates.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
